  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 116 PM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\n*** THROUGH SUNDAY ***\n\nAs noted above, the upper pattern will stay progressive, with\nmultiple upper waves expected to eject onto the Plains over the \nnext week. The first of these will be the upper low currently \nlocated off the coast of the Pacific NW. In advance of this wave, \nlee sfc pressure falls will result in southerly sfc winds which \nhelp gradually warm temps through Sat, peaking in the mid 60s to \nlow 70s on Saturday. However, a cold frontal passage on Saturday \nwill result in sharply cooler temps by Sun, with highs in the \nupper 40s and low 50s (which is 10-15 degrees below normal). Most \nguidance has the front located off the Caprock Sat PM, and there \nis at least a low chance of showers/storms with this boundary. \nPWAT values, while not high, are expected to be above 0.75\" which \nis still above normal for mid-November. The latest model blend \nkept the PoPs just below mentionable levels (less than 15%), but \nit would not be a surprise if we need to introduce a slight chance\nof precip off the Caprock if current trends hold.\n\n*** COOLER, UNSETTLED PATTERN FOR THANKSGIVING WEEK ***\n\nAnother fast-moving wave is expected to track onto the Plains late\nMon or Tues. Models still vary with the strength of this upper \ntrough, but there is sufficient signal to warrant a chance of \nprecip in our forecast area Mon night into Tues (20-40% chance). \nGiven the nocturnal timing of this system and lows currently \nforecast to be in the low/mid 30s, will need to keep an eye on \nprecip type. The most likely scenario is still for precip to be \nall rain, but a small percentage (5-10%) of ensemble members do \nhave snow mixing in. While the details become fuzzy in the \nextended as model solutions diverge, the active southern jet \nstream is expected to persist through the end of November. This \nmeans there is potential for additional systems on or around \nThanksgiving. While precip chances (and type) are difficult to pin\ndown this far in advance, there is high confidence that temps \nremain below normal next week, with highs likely to stay in the \n40s and 50s. Lows may occasionally dip below freezing on the \nCaprock, with low temps forecast to be in the low to mid 30s from\nSunday onward.",
